================================================================================
WHAT THE MIT GRANT ABOVE COVERS — AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
================================================================================

This repository holds four different kinds of material, and one licence cannot
honestly cover all of them. The boundary is stated by PATH rather than by
adjective, so that somebody deciding whether they may reuse a file does not
have to guess.

**Section 1 is the default.** Anything tracked in this repository that is not
named in sections 2, 3 or 4 is under the MIT grant above. That rule, rather
than the list, is what makes the boundary complete — a list alone goes stale
the next time a file is added, and anything a list forgets falls back to all
rights reserved, which is the condition this file exists to end.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. CODE, SPECIFICATIONS AND FIRST-PARTY OUTPUT — MIT (the grant above)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Everything not listed in sections 2, 3 or 4, including:

    src/**            the application, the scorers, the reference implementations
    scripts/**        the data pipeline, generators, loaders and gates
    functions/**      the Cloud Functions serving layer
    ai/**             the assistant's tools and orchestration
    vite/**           first-party build plugins
    tests/**          the end-to-end and SEO suites
    docs/**           plans, specifications and the methodology handbook
    .claude/**        ingest skills and operator runbooks
    .github/**        CI workflows
    state/**          pipeline and watcher state
    data-reports/**   generated daily reports

    index.html, vite.config.ts, vite.config.ai.ts, vitest.config.ts,
    vitest.setup.ts, tsconfig*.json, tailwind.config.js, postcss.config.js,
    eslint.config.js, playwright.config.ts, components.json, package.json,
    package-lock.json, docker-compose.yml, firebase.json, firestore.rules
                      build, test, lint and deployment configuration

    METHODOLOGY.md, README.md, CLAUDE.md and the other root documents

    public/**         EXCEPT public/fonts/** and public/images/IMG_*.png —
                      the tracked tree here is this project's own output: the
                      generated sitemaps, llms.txt, the article pages, the
                      og-image cards and the app icons. (The election corpus
                      that once lived here is gitignored and bucket-served; it
                      is data, and section 2 governs it wherever it appears.)

The build configuration is listed explicitly because leaving it out was not a
cosmetic gap: `src/**` is a strict-mode Vite application and cannot be built,
typechecked, linted or tested without those files. A grant that offers the
application while withholding the means to build it grants nothing.

Use it, fork it, ship it, sell it. No permission needed and none should be
asked for. That is deliberate: the procurement risk methodology in particular
is published so that other people — journalists, researchers, auditors, other
platforms — can check it, argue with it, and reuse it without a conversation.

Note on `docs/**`: the MIT grant covers this project's authorship. Where a
document quotes an external source (a study, a directive, a ministry report),
the quoted material remains its own author's and is reproduced here under
ordinary quotation; MIT cannot and does not relicense it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. REPUBLISHED PUBLIC DATA — each source's own terms, NOT MIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    data/**
    raw_data/**
    any data corpus served from the storage bucket

These trees are overwhelmingly Bulgarian and EU public-sector information
republished from its original holders — the Central Election Commission, the
Agency for Public Procurement / ЦАИС ЕОП, the Commerce Registry, the National
Audit Office, ИСУН, ДФ „Земеделие", НЗОК, НСИ, Eurostat, КЗП and others. We
did not create it and cannot relicense it. Each source's terms, and any
peculiarities of its reuse conditions, are documented per source in the "Data
sources" section of README.md.

Derived artifacts computed from that data (aggregates, rollups, payloads) are
distributed on the same footing as their inputs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THIRD-PARTY LICENSED MATERIAL — its own licence, NOT MIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    public/fonts/**   Inter and Fraunces, self-hosted from Google Fonts under
    video/public/fonts/**
                      the SIL Open Font License 1.1. The .css files beside them
                      are generated by scripts/fonts/fetch-fonts.mjs and are
                      MIT; the .woff2 files are not ours to relicense.

    vendor/*.tgz      a repacked upstream npm package (kdtree-ts), redistributed
                      under its own upstream licence. See vendor/README.md for
                      why it is vendored.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. BRAND, LIKENESS AND PHOTOGRAPHS — all rights reserved
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    brand/**          the Наясно logo, wordmark, ad creative and channel art
    assets/**         photographs of identifiable people
    public/images/IMG_*.png
                      the same, as shipped
    video/** (except video/public/fonts/**)
                      video assets built from the above

Forking the code does not carry the right to use the project's name, logo or
visual identity to present the fork as this project, and photographs of
identifiable people carry personality rights no software licence can transfer.
Replace them with your own.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. NOT A FINDING — the disclaimer that travels with the risk methodology
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The procurement risk flags, the contract risk index (CRI), the per-contract
grade and the entity exposure grades are DOCUMENTATION AND PATTERN SIGNALS.
They are computed by a public publisher, not by an institution with a legal
mandate to investigate, and a flag that fires means only that a contract or an
entity matches a pattern worth a second look.

The Open Contracting Partnership's framing is adopted verbatim: behaviour that
trips a flag may be "a) not at all illicit or suboptimal; b) not illicit, but
suboptimal in terms of value for money…; or c) illicit." That ordering names
the two innocent explanations before the guilty one; it is not a claim about
which is more frequent, and the published research is explicit that validated
red flags correlate weakly with corruption at the level of a single contract.

Nothing in this repository is a finding of wrongdoing about any named person or
company, and none of it is fit for use as the basis of a legal conclusion.
Reuse it accordingly.
